{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Check your inbox{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Check your inbox</h1>

    {# DELIBERATELY IDENTICAL FOR EVERY VISITOR, AND DELIBERATELY ADDRESS-FREE (AC-36).

       This page is where *every* submission of the forgot-password form lands — the account that
       exists, the address nobody registered, the account already at its hourly cap, and the address
       the `Email` value object refuses — and it must look identical from all four. "We sent a link to
       name@example.com" would be friendlier and would break the design: the address would have to
       arrive here somehow (session, flash, query string), and a page that varies at all between the
       four cases reintroduces the enumeration oracle the controller exists to close, at the very last
       step.

       It also has to survive a direct GET from somebody who never submitted anything, since it is an
       anonymous URL anyone can type. The one variable it renders — `lifetime`, see below — is derived
       from a compile-time domain constant and is the same string for everybody, so there is still
       nothing here that could leak or distinguish.

       Unstyled, like the rest of slices 1–3. ADR-0006's SCSS system arrives with the first real
       layout; marking this up by hand now would only be thrown away then. #}
    <p>If that address belongs to an account, a message with a link for setting a new password is on
        its way. Open the link and choose the password you want.</p>

    {# `lifetime` is a phrase built from `PasswordResetRequest::LIFETIME_SECONDS` by
       `LifetimePhrase`, the same helper both reset mail templates use — **not** the words "one hour"
       typed here. This paragraph and the mail are two surfaces stating one fact, and a literal here
       would go stale the moment the constant changed, silently, with every test still green: no
       assertion connects a paragraph of prose to a domain constant, and nobody re-reads this page
       when editing an aggregate. `TwigPasswordResetMailer` argues at length why the constant has to
       be the only place that number exists; this page is inside that argument, not an exception to
       it.

       This is the variable the note above refers to, and it is not an exception to it: it depends on
       a constant, not on the request, so the four AC-5 cases still render byte-identically. #}
    <p>The link works once and expires after {{ lifetime }}. Asking for another link cancels the
        previous one, so use the most recent message if you have asked more than once.</p>

    <p>Nothing arrived? It can take a minute, and it is worth checking your spam folder — then
        <a href="{{ path('app_forgot_password') }}">ask us to send another one</a>.</p>

    <p><a href="{{ path('app_login') }}">Back to sign in</a></p>
{% endblock %}
